{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01BVWXXnxSnoEgKByCNPNUmD","type":"tool_result","content":"229:def vs_of_layout(g, order):\n230-    \"\"\"Kinnersley p. 346: `max_{1 <= i < |V|} |V_L(i)|`, `V_L(i)` the vertices\n231-    at positions <= i with a neighbour at a position > i.\"\"\"\n232-    n, adj = g\n233-    best = 0\n234-    masks = _prefix_masks(order)\n235-    for i in range(n - 1):\n236-        pre = masks[i]\n237-        cnt = sum(1 for v in order[: i + 1] if adj[v] & ~pre)\n238-        best = max(best, cnt)\n239-    return best\n240-\n241-\n242-def vertex_separation(g):\n243-    n, _ = g\n\"\"\"loop0014 item 03: the linear reductions into vertex separation, checked\nagainst brute force on small graphs (the full atlas run is\n`python -m learning.subexp_reductions`).\"\"\"\nimport itertools\n\nimport pytest\n\nfrom learning import subexp_reductions as sr\nfrom paper1.complex_check import cutwidth_dp, graph, modified_cutwidth_dp, vertex_separation\n\n\ndef _graphs(nmax):\n    return sr.atlas(nmax)\n\n\ndef test_subset_dp_matches_permutations():\n    for g in _graphs(5):\n        assert sr.vs(g) == vertex_separation(g)\n\n\ndef test_mosp_pattern_dp_matches_literal_definition():\n    rows, cols = 3, 4\n    seen = 0\n    for M in sr.all_matrices(rows, cols):\n        if seen >= 300 or not any(any(r) for r in M):\n            continue\n        seen += 1\n        assert sr.mosp_subset_dp(M) == sr.mosp_permutations(M)\n\n\ndef test_cobipartite_double_adds_n():\n    for g in _graphs(5):\n        assert sr.vs(sr.cobipartite_double(g)) == sr.vs(g) + g[0]\n        assert sr.is_cobipartite(sr.cobipartite_double(g))\n\n\ndef test_clique_blowup_multiplies_pw_plus_one():\n    for g in _graphs(5):\n        assert sr.vs(sr.clique_blowup(g, 2)) + 1 == 2 * (sr.vs(g) + 1)\n\n#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"Regression gate for loop0014 (pathwidth after ETH).\n\nPasses only if all five hold:\n1. `lake build` in lean/ succeeds;\n2. every `.lean` file under lean/MOSPFormalization/ (top level, Complex/ and\n   Search/) is imported by lean/MOSPFormalization.lean, so the build really\n   checks it;\n3. the number of `sorry` in Lean *code* (comments and docstrings stripped) is\n   at most the baseline (0: the development has none since Appendix E) plus\n   the entries in allowed_sorries.txt, and no `axiom` declaration exists;\n4. `python -m pytest tests/ -q -x` passes, and so does `pytest` in\n   pathwidth_solver/;\n5. the paper builds with no undefined references (`make -C paper1/latex check`).\n\n    python3 Ralph_Loops/loop0014/gate.py\n\"\"\"\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nimport os\nimport re\nimport signal\nimport subprocess\nimport sys\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\nHERE = Path(__file__).resolve().parent\nREPO = HERE.parents[1]\nLEAN = REPO / \"lean\"\nBASELINE_SORRIES = 0\nALLOWED = HERE / \"allowed_sorries.txt\"\nPAPER = REPO / \"paper1\" / \"latex\"\n\n\ndef strip_comments(src: str) -> str:\n    out, i, depth = [], 0, 0\n    while i < len(src):\n        if src.startswith(\"/-\", i):\n            depth += 1; i += 2; continue\n        if depth and src.startswith(\"-/\", i):\n            depth -= 1; i += 2; continue\n        if depth:\n            i += 1; continue\n        if src.startswith(\"--\", i):\n            j = src.find(\"\\n\", i); i = len(src) if j < 0 else j; continue\n        if src[i] == '\"':\n            j = i + 1\n            while j < len(src) and src[j] != '\"':\n                j += 2 if src[j] == \"\\\\\" else 1\n            i = j + 1; continue\n        out.append(src[i]); i += 1\n    return \"\".join(out)\n\n\ndef _run(cmd: list[str], cwd: Path, timeout: float) -> subprocess.CompletedProcess:\n    \"\"\"`subprocess.run(cmd, capture_output=True, timeout=timeout)` with two\n    differences, both from the loop0014 item 01 gate hang (2026-11-02): the\n    command runs in its own session, so that on timeout its whole process group\n    is killed (`subprocess.run` kills only the direct child, and the forked\n    pytest worker that had hung outlived the gate by an hour), and the output\n    produced before the timeout travels with the exception, so that `main` can\n    print which test was running.\"\"\"\n    proc = subprocess.Popen(cmd, cwd=cwd, stdout=subprocess.PIPE, stderr=subprocess.STDOUT,\n                            text=True, errors=\"replace\", start_new_session=True)\n    try:\n        out, _ = proc.communicate(timeout=timeout)\n    except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:\n        os.killpg(proc.pid, signal.SIGKILL)\n        out, _ = proc.communicate()\n        raise subprocess.TimeoutExpired(cmd, timeout, output=out)\n    return subprocess.CompletedProcess(cmd, proc.returncode, out, \"\")\n\n\n# A test that runs this long has every thread's stack dumped to stderr (pytest's\n# faulthandler plugin); the test goes on, and the dump names it if the gate\n# then times out. The suite takes about 5 min in all.\nFAULTHANDLER = \"faulthandler_timeout=900\"\n\n\ndef _main() -> int:\n\"\"\"Two-ended search: the state lattice of a refutation, forward and backward.\n\nA closing order `c_1 … c_n` of the customers is feasible for \"MOSP ≤ k\"\nexactly when every prefix `S_t = {c_1 … c_t}` satisfies `|N(S_t) ∖ S_t| ≤ k − 1`\n(`satisfiability/customer_search.py`: closing `c` from `S` costs\n`|N[S ∪ c] ∖ S| = |N(S ∪ c) ∖ (S ∪ c)| + 1`). So \"MOSP ≤ k\" is reachability\nfrom `∅` to `V` inside the family `F_k = {S : |N(S) ∖ S| ≤ k − 1}` by single\nadditions. Reversing the production sequence maps closing orders to closing\norders of the same graph (the opening order reversed), so it gives the same\nsearch; the genuinely different direction is building the order from its\n**end**: states are suffixes `T = V ∖ S`, walked from `V` by single removals\nunder the same membership test, `|∂_in(T)| ≤ k − 1`.\n\nThis module enumerates both frontiers exactly and rule-free: `R_f(i)`, the\nsets of size `i` reachable from `∅` inside `F_k`, and `R_b(i)`, those from\nwhich `V` is