{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01LjhZRof94JEaWNA5ZoWgg6","type":"tool_result","content":"[exited with code 0]\n======\n### 2.2 Trees of cliques: block graphs (polynomial) against octopus graphs (NP-hard)\n\n- **Status.** Pathwidth is polynomial on block graphs (Chou et al. 2008) and\n  NP-hard on octopus graphs and weighted trees (Mihai & Todinca 2009, Thms 3–4).\n  Both families are natural MOSP instances (§1, MOSP side). Mihai & Todinca leave\n  two questions open (p. 10): **is pathwidth polynomial on octopus graphs with\n  a constant number of legs, or with legs of constant length?** They also ask\n  whether pathwidth can be approximated within a constant factor on chordal\n  graphs or on weighted trees.\n- **Opportunity.** (a) A block-graph MOSP generator whose optimum is known in\n  polynomial time at any size, which tests the customer search and every bound\n  far beyond 125 customers. It would be the first MOSP benchmark with certified\n  optima at 10³–10⁴ customers. (b) Octopus instances generated from Mihai &\n  Todinca's balanced 0-1 systems as a *provably hard* MOSP family with row sums\n  ≤ 2. (c) Their open questions are concrete and small enough to probe with the\n  exact solver: run octopuses with 3–4 legs and look for a DP or a\n  counterexample to leg contiguity. This is the family `reports/ml_nature.md` §6\n  and §38 identified as defeating the bounds, so a polynomial algorithm on\n  block graphs is also a candidate *bound*: exact pathwidth of a spanning\n  block-graph subgraph is a valid lower bound by subgraph monotonicity.\n\n### 2.3 Outerplanar, cactus, Halin, series–parallel: polynomial in theory, no practical exact algorithm\n======\n300:## 10. The search from the other end, and the meet in the band: dead, by asymmetry\n301-\n302-*(2026-11-04, same day; `learning/two_ended.py`, 14 tests in\n303-`tests/test_two_ended.py`; `reports/scratch/checkpoint/two_ended/`.)*\n304-\n305-§9 proposed a search from the other end, or a meet in the band with the two\n306-frontiers stored, because the search's states peak before the band its\n307-certificate needs. Measured rule-free on the 64 instances at 30–50, the\n308-idea dies on a one-line fact about the state lattice.\n309-\n310-**The lattice.** A closing order is feasible for \"MOSP ≤ k\" exactly when\n311-every prefix `S` has `|N(S) ∖ S| ≤ k − 1` (closing `c` from `S` costs\n312-`|N[S ∪ c] ∖ S| = |N(S ∪ c) ∖ (S ∪ c)| + 1`, `satisfiability/customer_search.py`).\n313-So the decision is reachability from `∅` to `V` inside\n314-`F_k = {S : |N(S) ∖ S| ≤ k − 1}` by single additions. Reversing the\n315-production sequence maps closing orders to closing orders of the same graph\n316-(the opening order, reversed), so the \"reversed\" search is the same search.\n317-The genuinely different direction builds the order from its **end**: start\n318-at `S = V`, remove the customer closed last, and require the prefix that\n319-remains to be in `F_k`. Forward frontier `R_f(i)`: the sets of size `i`\n320-reachable from `∅`; backward frontier `R_b(i)`: those from which `V` is\n321-reachable. Both enumerated exactly (bitmask layers; the backward one keeps\n322-a per-vertex count of neighbours still inside, updated for all candidates\n323-at once), verified against a subset DP on 485 (graph, k) cases.\n324-\n325-**The asymmetry.** `|N(S) ∖ S| ≤ |V ∖ S|`: a set missing at most `k − 1`\n326-vertices is feasible whatever it is, and so is every set between it and\n327-`V`. Hence `R_b(n − j) = C(n, j)` for every `j ≤ k − 1`, before any\n328-constraint bites: the last `k − 1` customers closed are unconstrained, and\n329-the backward search must choose them blind. At 30 customers and `k = 11`\n330-the sixth backward layer has 2,035,800 states; the enumeration hit its\n=====\n51:| **Split** (clique K, independent I) | **ω − 1 or ω** (Gustedt Lemma 2.8). Ours: **ω − 1 iff some x, y ∈ K (x = y allowed) have every p ∈ I non-adjacent to x or to y**, where K is a maximum clique with V∖K independent | O(n³) (Gustedt, k = 1 of Thm 7.1); the criterion takes O(ω²·\\|I\\|) | Gustedt 1993 Lemma 2.8, p. 237; p. 235 (read). Criterion ours, proof in §2.2; checked 300/300 and on the corpus | **2,401 / 122**, criterion = opt on all |\n76:| **Primitive starlike** (peripheral cliques pairwise disjoint) | **O(n²)** | generalised partition, pseudo-polynomial DP | **Gustedt Thm 5.8, p. 243 (read)** | see §2 (S2) |\n77:| **k-starlike** (each peripheral clique has ≤ k vertices outside the centre) | **O(n^(2k+1))** | DP over sorted decompositions | **Gustedt Thm 7.1, p. 246 (read)** | see §2 |\n78:| Starlike, small centre | O(4^(α₀)·r + n), α₀ = central clique size (FPT in α₀) | Algorithm 6.3 | **Gustedt Thm 6.4, p. 246 (read)** | see §2 |\n79:| Octopus, every leg's separators of equal size | polynomial | reduce to primitive starlike | **Mihai & Todinca 2009 Thm 5, author copy p. 10 (read)** | — |\n96:| **Chordal** | **Gustedt Thm 4.1, p. 239 (read)** |\n97:| **Starlike** (a central clique, every other maximal clique meets only it) | **Gustedt Cor. 4.3, p. 241 (read)** |\n123:  bounded leafage**. The last is the natural parameter between starlike (hard)\n149:| **Central pattern**: a pattern P₀ contains every customer who needs ≥ 2 patterns (customers outside P₀ need exactly one) | starlike (chordal, star clique tree centred at P₀) | **NP-hard** in general; **O(4^\\|P₀\\|·r + n)**; **O(n^(2k+1))** if every other pattern has ≤ k customers outside P₀; **O(n²)** if the other patterns are pairwise disjoint | ours (bags P₀, P_i form a clique star decomposition); **Gustedt Cor. 4.3, Thms 6.4, 7.1, 5.8 (read)**. Gustedt's hardness instance *is* such a matrix: P₀ = V(G), one pattern per edge {u, w} plus n private customers. Corpus: **5** instances (all Simonis) |\n150:| …with k = 1: every other pattern has exactly one customer outside P₀ | split | **∈ {ω, ω + 1}**, decided by the two-vertex criterion | Gustedt Lemma 2.8; criterion ours |\n201: