he claims. One refuter subagent read the","+  four proofs and Corollary 4.5's arithmetic blind to the rest of this","+  section, with its own brute force to 6 vertices: Theorems 4.1, 4.3 and","+  the single move of 4.4 sound and agreeing with its brute force;","+  Corollary 4.2's index range wrong as first written (fixed and now","+  checked); the induction of 4.4 wrong in its order (fixed); Lemma 4.6","+  needing the uniformity hypothesis (added, with the path-graph","+  argument); the constant of the verified route unstated (now `1`); and","+  the paper-route table mixing the two budgets (recomputed per route).","+  Every point is folded in above. What it did not verify: Gustedt's","+  construction and the 3-regular remark, which are read from the held","+  paper (pp. 239–241)."," - That any of this is in Lean: the item is mathematics; the twin lemma","   and the cobipartite normal form are stated against layouts exactly as","   `VertexSeparation.lean` defines them, so item 08 or the reserve could"]}]},{"filePath":"/home/al/dev/MOSP/Ralph_Loops/loop0014/session_it04.jsonl","hunks":[{"oldStart":842,"oldLines":3,"newStart":842,"newLines":6,"lines":[" {\"type\":\"system\",\"subtype\":\"thinking_tokens\",\"estimated_tokens\":435,\"estimated_tokens_delta\":35,\"session_id\":\"28999536-73fd-4001-9759-0375517393ee\",\"uuid\":\"35652772-4809-49e8-8e85-57197e6c856a\"}"," {\"type\":\"assistant\",\"message\":{\"model\":\"claude-fable-5-1\",\"id\":\"msg_011CgibiefkyKTLNkzUHPWAS\",\"type\":\"message\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"thinking\",\"thinking\":\"\",\"signature\":\"\"}],\"container\":null,\"stop_reason\":null,\"stop_sequence\":null,\"stop_details\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":32,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":5210,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":214981,\"cache_creation\":{\"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens\":5210,\"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens\":0},\"output_tokens\":2,\"service_tier\":\"standard\",\"inference_geo\":\"global\",\"speed\":\"standard\"},\"input_transformations\":[],\"diagnostics\":null,\"context_management\":null},\"parent_tool_use_id\":null,\"session_id\":\"28999536-73fd-4001-9759-0375517393ee\",\"uuid\":\"ce853be7-f9b0-4a05-a6dd-e651c22b836b\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-11-04T21:29:39.251Z\",\"request_id\":\"req_011CgibieCyqeb17N69YQ7dA\",\"thinking_duration_ms\":6622,\"thinking_display\":\"updates\"}"," {\"type\":\"assistant\",\"message\":{\"model\":\"claude-fable-5-1\",\"id\":\"msg_011CgibiefkyKTLNkzUHPWAS\",\"type\":\"message\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"id\":\"toolu_013QixQii5FFJekgui8SmDzQ\",\"name\":\"Bash\",\"input\":{\"command\":\"(nice -n 19 python3 -m learning.subexp_classes --nmax 7 --patterns-max 8 > reports/scratch/subexp_pathwidth/04_classes/check_atlas7.log 2>&1; echo \\\"exit $?\\\" >> reports/scratch/subexp_pathwidth/04_classes/check_atlas7.log) &\\npython3 - <<'EOF'\\np='reports/subexp_pathwidth.md'\\ns=open(p).read()\\ndef rep(old,new,count=1):\\n    global s\\n    assert s.count(old)==count, (s.count(old), old[:60])\\n    s=s.replace(old,new)\\n\\n# log conventions, once, in 4.0\\nrep('''in §4.7.\\n\\n### 4.1 Cobipartite''','''in §4.7. Conventions: `log` in the budgets of §1.3.8 is natural, as in\\nthe paper; bit counts are `log₂`; the numbers below mix the two as\\ntheir formulas do.\\n\\n### 4.1 Cobipartite''')\\n\\n# Thm 4.1 converse\\nrep('''For `j ≤ t < |Y|` the cut after the `t`-th vertex of `Y` lies at or\\nafter `p`, so all of `X` is placed and the cut counts exactly\\n`t + |N_X(Y^τ_{>t})| =: g(t)`; so `v ≥ g(t)` for `t ≥ j`.''','''For `t = j` take the cut just after `p` (placed: `X` and the first `j`\\nvertices of `τ`), and for `j < t < |Y|` the cut after the `t`-th vertex\\nof `Y`, which lies after `p`; in both all of `X` is placed, and the cut\\ncounts exactly `t + |N_X(Y^τ_{>t})| =: g(t)`; so `v ≥ g(t)` for `t ≥ j`.''')\\n\\n# Cor 4.2 range\\nrep('''there is a chain `∅ = S_0 ⊂ S_1 ⊂ … ⊂ S_{|Y|} = Y`, `|S_s| = s`, with\\n`|N_X(S_s)| − s ≤ c` for every `s < |Y|` (`S_s` being the suffix set\\n`Y^τ_{>|Y|−s}`).''','''there is a chain `∅ = S_0 ⊂ S_1 ⊂ … ⊂ S_{|Y|} = Y`, `|S_s| = s`, with\\n`|N_X(S_s)| − s ≤ c` for every `1 ≤ s ≤ |Y|` (`S_s` being the suffix set\\n`Y^τ_{>|Y|−s}`, `s = |Y| − t`; `S_0 = ∅` is unconstrained — the first\\ndraft wrote `s < |Y|`, which the refuter's brute force rejected on 42\\ncases, e.g. `K₃` with `|A| = 1`, `k = 1`; the corrected range is checked\\non every `(partition, k)` to 7 vertices, §4.8).''')\\n\\n# twin induction\\nrep('''Repeating for the members of a class in layout order, each moved to\\njust before the next, makes the class consecutive without separating\\nany other class (a vertex is inserted next to a vertex outside that\\nclass). ∎''','''For a class `x_1, …, x_w` in layout order, move `x_{w−1}` to just\\nbefore `x_w`, then `x_{w−2}` to just before `x_{w−1}`, and so on down to\\n`x_1` (in *reverse* layout order: moving `x_1` first and then `x_2`\\nwould strand `x_1`, as the refuter's brute force showed on 4,260\\ncases); the class becomes consecutive, and no other class is separated,\\nsince each insertion is next to a vertex outside that class. ∎''')\\n\\n# Lemma 4.6 uniformity\\nrep('''**Lemma 4.6 (bits).** `N ≥ log₂ ( n! / (g!)^{n/g} ) ≥ n log₂ ( n / (e g) )`.\\n\\n*Proof.* Distinct checkpoint sequences of a layout are at least the\\nmultinomial `n!/(g!)^{n/g}` (choose which `g` vertices fill each\\nsegment), each assignment determines at most one, and\\n`n! ≥ (n/e)^n`, `g! ≤ g^g`. ∎''','''**Lemma 4.6 (bits).** If the decoding of checkpoints from assignments\\nis the same for every graph on `n` vertices (a certificate *scheme*,\\nnot one per graph), then `N ≥ log₂ ( n! / (g!)^{n/g} ) − 1 ≥\\nn log₂ ( n / (e g) ) − 1`.\\n\\n*Proof.* Distinct checkpoint sequences number the multinomial\\n`n!/(g!)^{n/g}` (choose which `g` vertices fill each segment). On the\\npath graph `P_n` with `k = 1` every layout is one of the two monotone\\norders, so a scheme that decodes the same way for every graph must\\nhave, for each sequence, an assignment decoding to it or to its\\nreversal — otherwise some relabelling of `P_n` has no certificate — so\\n`2^N ≥ ½ · n!/(g!)^{n/g}`; and `n! ≥ (n/e)^n`, `g! ≤ g^g`. (Without\\nuniformity the lemma fails: a fixed graph may need no bits at all.) ∎''')\\n\\n# verified route constant\\nrep('''`Θ(g²)` variables per segment, `Θ(ng)` in all. So\\n`N ≥ max( n log₂(n/(eg)), c·ng )` for some `c > 0`, and no `g` fits:\\nfor `g ≥ √log n` the second term is `Ω(n √log n)`, above the budget\\n`n √log n / (log log n)²` for every `n ≥ 16`; for `g < √log n` the\\nfirst is `≥ n (log₂ n − ½ log₂ log n − 1.45)`, which at `n = 125` is\\nalready `4.4 n` against a budget of `0.89 n` and grows like `n log n`.''','''`g²` variables per segment (the nested chain's `y[v, t]` on the\\nsegment's `g` vertices and `g` steps), `ng` in all. So\\n`N ≥ max( n log₂(n/(eg)) − 1, ng )`, and no `g` fits: for\\n`g ≥ √log n` the second term is `≥ n √log n`, above the budget\\n`n √log n / (log log n)²` for every `n ≥ 16` (where `(log log n)² ≥ 1`);\\nfor `g < √log n` the first is `≥ n (log₂ n − ½ log₂ log n − 1.45) − 1`,\\nwhich at `n = 125` is already `4.4 n` against a budget of `0.89 n` and\\ngrows like `n log n`.''')\\n\\n# paper route\\nrep('''`S_{(i+1)g}` given `S_{ig}`, at least `log₂ C(n − ig, g) ≥ g log₂(n/g) −\\nO(g)` of them on the first segments; and Lemma 4.6 bounds the wire count\\n`s ≥ N`. Taking the smallest `g` whose bit count meets the budget,\\n`g = n / (e · 2^{β})`, `β = √log n/(log log n)²`, the segment width\\n`g log₂(n/g) ≈ n β 2^{−β}` must be at most `s^{9/10} ≤ (n √log n)^{9/10}`,\\ni.e. `2^{−β} β ≤ n^{−1/10} (log n)^{0.45}`, which fails for large `n`\\nsince `β = o(log n)`. The arithmetic (`gap_table`; the row is the\\nsegment width over `s^{9/10}` at the smallest fitting `g`):\\n\\n| `n` | budget, bits | `g/n` | segment width | `s^{9/10}` | ratio |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| 125 | 111 | 0.20 | 58 | 157 | 0.37 |\\n| 10³ | 704 | 0.23 | 485 | 1,200 | 0.41 |\\n| 10⁴ | 6,160 | 0.24 | 4,940 | 10,800 | 0.46 |\\n| 10⁶ | 5.39 × 10⁵ | 0.25 | 5.02 × 10⁵ | 8.19 × 10⁵ | 0.61 |\\n| 10⁹ | 4.95 × 10⁸ | 0.26 | 5.06 × 10⁸ | 4.92 × 10⁸ | 1.03 |\\n\\nSo the paper route is excluded for this family **asymptotically only**:\\nthe ratio passes 1 near `n = 10⁹`, and below that the arithmetic\\nalone does not exclude a coarse chain — with the caveat that Theorem\\nD.1's constants are unknown (§1.3.8), so nothing is claimed at any\\nfinite `n` in either direction. The budget `n √log n/(log log n)²` is\\nitself below `n` until `n ≈ 10⁹`, which is the same fact: the verified\\nexponent is `o(n)` only in the limit.''','''`S_{(i+1)g}` given `S_{ig}`, at least `log₂ C(n − ig, g) ≥ g log₂(n/g) −\\nO(g)` of them on the first segments; and Lemma 4.6 bounds the wire count\\n`s ≥ N`. This route has its own budget, `s ≤ B_p = n √log n`\\n(§1.3.8), not the verified one. Taking the smallest `g` whose bit count\\nmeets it, `g = n / (e · 2^{√log n})`, the segment width\\n`g log₂(n/g) ≈ 1.44 · n √log n · 2^{−√log n}` must be at most\\n`s^{9/10} ≤ B_p^{9/10}`, i.e. `2^{−√log n} ≤ n^{−1/10} (log n)^{−0.05}`\\nup to constants, which fails for large `n` since `√log n = o(log n)`\\n— but only for very large `n`. The arithmetic (`gap_table`; the ratio\\nis the segment width over `B_p^{9/10}` at the smallest fitting `g`,\\nwith the verified route's `g` beside it for comparison):\\n\\n| `n` | verified `B_v/n` | its `g/n` | paper `B_p/n` | its `g/n` | segment width | `B_p^{9/10}` | ratio |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| 125 | 0.89 | 0.20 | 2.20 | 0.080 | 36.5 | 157 | 0.23 |\\n| 10³ | 0.70 | 0.23 | 2.63 | 0.060 | 242 | 1,200 | 0.20 |\\n| 10⁶ | 0.54 | 0.25 | 3.72 | 0.028 | 1.44 × 10⁵ | 8.19 × 10⁵ | 0.18 |\\n| 10⁹ | 0.50 | 0.26 | 4.55 | 0.016 | 9.4 × 10⁷ | 4.92 × 10⁸ | 0.19 |\\n| 10¹⁵ | 0.47 | 0.27 | 5.88 | 0.006 | 4.6 × 10¹³ | 1.56 × 10¹⁴ | 0.29 |\\n| 10²¹ | 0.46 | 0.27 | 6.95 | 0.003 | 2.5 × 10¹⁹ | 4.6 × 10¹⁹ | 0.55 |\\n| 10²⁴ | 0.46 | 0.27 | 7.43 | 0.002 | 1.9 × 10²² | 2.4 × 10²² | 0.78 |\\n\\nSo the paper route is excluded for this family **asymptotically only**:\\nthe ratio is below 1 at every `n` up to `10²⁴` and passes it beyond,\\nso at any size one could write down the arithmetic alone does not\\nexclude a coarse chain on the paper route — with the caveat that\\nTheorem D.1's constants are unknown (§1.3.8), so nothing is claimed at\\nany finite `n` in either direction. (The first draft mixed the routes,\\ntaking `g` from the verified budget and the width against `s = B_p`,\\nand put the crossover near `10⁹`; the refuter caught the mix.) The\\nverified budget `B_v` is itself below `n` at every `n` in the table,\\nwhich is the same fact: the verified exponent is `o(n)` only in the\\nlimit.''')\\n\\n# 4.7 table row for gap lemma\\nrep('''| any chain-carrying certificate at spacing `g` | — | `N ≥ n log₂(n/(eg))` (Lemma 4.6) | **no** on the verified route at every `n`; on the paper route asymptotically |''',\\n'''| any chain-carrying certificate at spacing `g` | — | `N ≥ n log₂(n/(eg)) − 1` (Lemma 4.6) | **no** on the verified route at every `n ≥ 16`; on the paper route asymptotically (crossover beyond `10²⁴`) |''')\\n\\n# 4.8 table: corollary row\\nrep('''| Thm 4.1, the layouts | the minimum over layouts with one clique entirely first (all orders of both sides) against `vs` | the same 496 pairs | **0** |''',\\n'''| Thm 4.1, the layouts | the minimum over layouts with one clique entirely first (all orders of both sides) against `vs` | the same 496 pairs | **0** |\\n| Cor. 4.2, the decision | the chain condition with the corrected range `1 ≤ s ≤ |Y|` against `vs ≤ k` | the same 496 pairs, every `0 ≤ k ≤ n`: 3,848 decisions | **0** (42 failures with the first draft's range) |''')\\nrep('''`tests/test_subexp_classes.py` (9 tests) runs''','''`tests/test_subexp_classes.py` (10 tests) runs''')\\nrep('''`tests/test_subexp_classes.py` (9); scratch''','''`tests/test_subexp_classes.py` (10